NAME
       lvreduce - make a logical volume smaller

SYNOPSIS
       lvreduce {-L [-]SIZE | -l [-]EXTENTS} [-r] [-f] LV

DESCRIPTION
       lvreduce shrinks a logical volume. The extents it frees go back to the
       volume group.

       Shrinking is risky: anything stored past the new end of the LV is
       lost. A file system must therefore be shrunk first, and only some
       file systems can shrink. ext4 can (resize2fs, unmounted). XFS cannot
       shrink at all: to make an XFS volume smaller, back up the files,
       recreate the LV and the file system, and restore.

       With -r, lvreduce shrinks the ext4 file system before the LV, and
       refuses when the file system cannot be shrunk. Without -r it checks
       too: it refuses when a file system on the LV is bigger than the new
       size. On an LV with no file system it warns that this may destroy
       data and asks before it goes on.

OPTIONS
       -L, --size [-]SIZE
              The new size, or with a minus sign the amount to remove. Units
              K, M, G, T.

       -l, --extents [-]N
              The same in extents.

       -r, --resizefs
              Shrink the file system first (ext4).

       -f, --force
              Do not ask for confirmation.

       -y, --yes
              Answer yes to all questions.

EXAMPLES
       Shrink an ext4 LV to 400 MiB, file system included:

           lvreduce -r -L 400M /dev/vgdata/archive

       Remove 200 MiB from an LV that holds no file system:

           lvreduce -f -L -200M vgdata/scratch

SEE ALSO
       lvextend(8), lvresize(8), resize2fs(8), lvm(8)
